"## Who's That Baller\n\nSeven mystery players. Players see the clues one at a time; club figures are league appearances and goals.\n\n| No. | Answer | Position \u00b7 nation | Youth club | Three clubs (apps / goals) | Caps (goals) | Career apps / goals | Honour | Award | Check |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 15 \u00b7 Mon 30 Nov | **Ruud Gullit** | Midfielder \u00b7 Netherlands | DWS | PSV 68/46; AC Milan 117/35; Chelsea 49/7 | 66 (17) | 463 / 166 | European Championship winner (1988) | Ballon d'Or (1987) | *(check)* League totals across Haarlem, Feyenoord, PSV, Milan, Sampdoria, Chelsea. |\n| 16 \u00b7 Tue 1 Dec | **Peter Schmeichel** | Goalkeeper \u00b7 Denmark | Gladsaxe-Hero | Br\u00f8ndby 119/2; Manchester United 292/0; Sporting CP 50/0 | 129 (1) | 650 / 11 | European Championship winner (1992) | IFFHS World's Best Goalkeeper (1992, 1993) | *(check)* Youth club; goals scored (he scored for Br\u00f8ndby, Aston Villa and Denmark); all totals. |\n| 17 \u00b7 Wed 2 Dec | **Gianfranco Zola** | Forward \u00b7 Italy | Nuorese | Napoli 105/32; Parma 102/49; Chelsea 229/59 | 35 (10) | 629 / 193 | UEFA Cup Winners' Cup winner (Chelsea) (1998) | FWA Footballer of the Year (1996\u201397) | *(check)* Youth club (Nuorese was his first senior club); league totals. |\n| 18 \u00b7 Thu 3 Dec | **Dennis Bergkamp** | Forward \u00b7 Netherlands | Ajax | Ajax 185/103; Inter 52/11; Arsenal 315/87 | 79 (37) | 552 / 201 | Premier League winner (1998, 2002, 2004) | PFA Players' Player of the Year (1997\u201398) | *(check)* League appearances and goals per club; career totals. |\n| 19 \u00b7 Fri 4 Dec | **Ian Rush** | Forward \u00b7 Wales | Chester | Chester 34/14; Liverpool 469/229; Juventus 29/7 | 73 (28) | 599 / 250 | European Cup winner (1984) | European Golden Shoe (1983\u201384) | *(check)* Career league totals including Leeds, Newcastle, Sheffield United and Wrexham. |\n| 20 \u00b7 Sat 5 Dec | **Fernando Torres** | Forward \u00b7 Spain | Atl\u00e9tico Madrid | Atl\u00e9tico Madrid 244/91; Liverpool 102/65; Chelsea 110/20 | 110 (38) | 501 / 182 | FIFA World Cup winner (2010) | Euro 2012 Golden Boot (2012) | *(check)* Atl\u00e9tico totals cover both spells; career totals include Milan and Sagan Tosu. |\n| 21 \u00b7 Sun 6 Dec | **Patrick Vieira** | Midfielder \u00b7 France | Cannes | Arsenal 279/29; Juventus 31/5; Inter 67/6 | 107 (6) | 456 / 45 | FIFA World Cup winner (1998) | Captain of Arsenal's unbeaten Invincibles (2003\u201304) | *(check)* League totals; the 'award' slot uses the Invincibles captaincy rather than an individual award. |\n\nReveal lines:\n\n- **No. 15, Ruud Gullit:** Dreadlocked captain of the Netherlands' Euro 88 winners who later became the first overseas manager to win the FA Cup.\n- **No. 16, Peter Schmeichel:** Captained Manchester United to the Champions League in his final game for the club, completing the 1999 Treble.\n- **No. 17, Gianfranco Zola:** Scored the Cup Winners' Cup final winner seconds after coming on as a substitute, and was later voted Chelsea's greatest ever player.\n- **No. 18, Dennis Bergkamp:** Arsenal's 'non-flying Dutchman', remembered for his touch, his vision and a famous spin-and-finish goal at Newcastle in 2002.\n- **No. 19, Ian Rush:** Liverpool's all-time record goalscorer, with 346 goals across two spells at the club.\n- **No. 20, Fernando Torres:** Scored the winner in the Euro 2008 final and once cost Chelsea a then-British record fee of \u00a350 million.\n- **No. 21, Patrick Vieira:** Born in Senegal, he became the driving force of Ars\u00e8ne Wenger's Arsenal and won the World Cup and the European Championship with France."
